# codescout retrieval stack — GPU profile (single CUDA card)
#
# !! THE MCP SERVER DOES NOT READ THIS FILE. Editing it will NOT change the
# !! running codescout you talk to from Claude Code. load_startup_env
# !! (src/config/global.rs:118-124) reads only $CODESCOUT_ENV_FILE or
# !! <global_config_dir>/.env, and by design "never reads the current working
# !! directory — a user-scoped server must not absorb an arbitrary repo's .env".
# !! On this host ~/.config/codescout/.env is a symlink to .env.amd, so .env.amd
# !! is the live file and this one is a template. Two further consequences:
# !!   - The global dotenv fills only keys the MCP env left UNSET, so a var set
# !!     in <profile>/.claude.json -> mcpServers/codescout/env can never be
# !!     overridden here; it has to be deleted from that JSON.
# !!   - Values below marked DISABLED are only disabled where this file is
# !!     actually loaded. Verify with `printenv` inside the server, not by
# !!     reading this file.
# !! See docs/issues/2026-07-25-env-copy-flow-stale-model-dir.md.
#
# Source this before running codescout / sync_project FROM THE CLI:
#   set -a; source .env.gpu; set +a
#
# Or pass to docker compose:
#   docker compose --profile gpu --env-file .env.gpu up -d

# Compose profile selector
CODESCOUT_RETRIEVAL_PROFILE=gpu

# Host directory holding the GGUF model file(s) — bind-mounted into dense-gpu
# and reranker-gpu at /models. Fetch/build both with:
#   ./scripts/fetch-models.sh --gpu
#   CodeRankEmbed-Q4_K_M.gguf         (90 MB)  — BUILT from nomic-ai/CodeRankEmbed.
#   bge-reranker-v2-m3-Q4_K_M.gguf    (419 MB) — gpustack/bge-reranker-v2-m3-GGUF
CODESCOUT_MODEL_DIR=./models

# ---- Codescout client wiring ----
CODESCOUT_QDRANT_URL=http://127.0.0.1:6334
CODESCOUT_EMBEDDER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:48081
CODESCOUT_EMBEDDER_MODEL_NAME=CodeRankEmbed-Q4_K_M.gguf
CODESCOUT_MODEL_DIM=768
# Q4_K_M is benchmarked best with NO query prefix (37, champion) — forcing the
# prefix drops to the f16+prefix tier (34). See docs/manual/src/concepts/
# retrieval-stack.md § Dense embedder: "We default to Q4 no-prefix."
# Uncomment only if switching the dense model to f16.
# CODESCOUT_QUERY_PREFIX="Represent this query for searching relevant code: "
CODESCOUT_SPARSE_EMBEDDER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:48084
# ---- Cross-encoder reranker: DISABLED (2026-07-28) ----
# Measured on the pinned 25-TC bench against one fixed dense-only index
# (bench_base_, 24,923 chunks), 4 runs per arm, back to back:
#
#            WITH rerank        WITHOUT rerank
#   score      32/75  (x4)        35/75  (x4)
#   p50      1994-2176 ms         51-61 ms        <- 33x to 42x
#
# CORRECTED 2026-07-28 — read this before acting on the numbers above.
#
# The LATENCY finding stands, but it indicts the SERVER, not reranking. The same
# model (bge-reranker-v2-m3) hosted on TEI was this project's champion at 37/75
# with p50 ~150 ms — see docs/trackers/retrieval-benchmark.md, 2026-05-12. The
# 1994-2176 ms above is llama-server/GGUF, which the 2026-07-27 OOM fix swapped
# in. So this is a ~13x HOSTING regression that the OOM fix introduced and nobody
# measured; it is not evidence that cross-encoder reranking is too expensive.
#
# The SCORE finding (-3) is WITHDRAWN. These arms differ from the 37/75 champion
# row in four dimensions at once — sparse off, boost 3.0 not 5.0, mode=full not
# code, llama-server not TEI — so they cannot attribute a delta to reranking.
# "Zero variance across 4 runs" was over-read too: the same arm later scored 37
# and then 35 while a background index saturated the GPU. Determinism holds only
# on a quiet box, so a bench run must first assert no indexer is running.
#
# Mechanism (unaffected by the above): SearchOpts::new sets overfetch = limit * 2
# and search_in reranks EVERY overfetched candidate, so limit=10 means 20 forward
# passes through a 568M-param XLM-RoBERTa-large cross-encoder on a 6 GiB card
# already hosting the dense server.
#
# Scope: measured with CODESCOUT_DISABLE_SPARSE=1 (below). Whether the tradeoff
# changes under sparse fusion is UNTESTED — re-measure if sparse is re-enabled.
# Next step is TEI with a capped --max-batch-tokens, not abandoning reranking.
# Full writeup: docs/issues/2026-07-28-reranker-costs-42x-latency-and-lowers-score.md
#
# The codescout-reranker-gpu container is left running so this is a one-line
# revert; search_in also degrades gracefully if the URL is unreachable.
#
# To re-enable: uncomment both lines below. reranker-gpu is llama-server (not
# TEI), so the client must post the {query, documents} / relevance_score shape —
# Protocol::Infinity in src/retrieval/reranker.rs. Dropping the PROTOCOL line
# silently falls back to the TEI shape and every rerank call fails.
# CODESCOUT_RERANKER_URL=http://127.0.0.1:48083
# CODESCOUT_RERANKER_PROTOCOL=llama-server
# ---- SPLADE sparse leg: DISABLED (2026-07-28) ----
# Search is pure dense ANN; the sparse leg is skipped at BOTH query and index
# time (src/retrieval/client.rs:39 folds this into the embedder's dense_only).
#
# Why: TEI/SPLADE peaked at ~3.0 GiB of this 6 GiB card. SPLADE projects every
# token to a [tokens x 30522] f16 vocab tensor, and TEI's default
# --max-batch-tokens 16384 makes that ~950 MiB per unfused copy (Candle does not
# fuse relu/log1p/max, so 2-3 copies are live at once).
#
# Measured cost: -2/75 on the pinned 25-TC bench at boost=3.0 (dense-only 32 vs
# fusion 34). Treat -2 as a LOWER bound — that run predates the fix for
# docs/issues/2026-07-02-tc-benchmark-harness-swallows-buffered-results.md,
# which suppressed hybrid arms more often than dense-only ones.
#
# Also stop the container:  docker stop codescout-sparse-gpu
#
# KEPT SET here, deliberately, while .env and .env.amd re-enabled sparse on
# 2026-08-07. This profile's note above describes a 6 GiB card, where ~2.7 GiB for
# SPLADE alongside the dense server is a real constraint. That is the case the flag
# exists for, and it is why sparse is flag-gated rather than simply switched on.
#
# ⚠ RECONCILE: the 6 GiB figure does not match the NVIDIA card on the desktop this
# was last edited from — an RTX A5000 with 24564 MiB, measured 2026-08-07 and
# essentially idle (45 MiB used). Either this profile targets the laptop, or the note
# is stale and sparse should be on here too. Whoever knows which host this profile is
# for should fix the note and, if it is the 24 GiB box, drop the line below.
#
# To re-enable: drop the line below and start sparse-gpu. A force reindex is NOT
# strictly required first — measured 2026-08-07 on the sibling amd profile, chunks
# written while this was set carry an EMPTY sparse vector (Qdrant accepts them; they
# simply never match) and that was only 7.7% of a 2000-point sample, which degrades
# gracefully to dense-only in RRF. Reindex to fill them when convenient.
CODESCOUT_DISABLE_SPARSE=1

# Inert while CODESCOUT_DISABLE_SPARSE=1. Kept for the re-enable path. MEASURED ON OUR
# SETUP, not a recommendation: 5.0 was the peak (35/75) on our corpus and dense model,
# 3.0 the long-standing default. Re-sweep with scripts/sweep-bm25-boost.sh before
# trusting either — see memory `conventions` § Environment-Agnostic Tuning.
CODESCOUT_BM25_BOOST=3.0


# Librarian artifact embedder — OpenAI-compatible /v1 endpoint on the same dense
# model as the retrieval stack. Read separately from CODESCOUT_EMBEDDER_* by the
# librarian, so it must be set here too (that split caused the 2026-07-10 outage).
LIBRARIAN_EMBED_MODEL=CodeRankEmbed
LIBRARIAN_EMBED_URL=http://127.0.0.1:48081/v1
